<?nvs
// This file, opened in VS Code with the Novis extension installed. Every comment
// points at something the editor is doing to the line under it — colour, a bar
// over a value, a glyph after a name — and none of it is decided here: the
// extension asks `nvs lsp` and draws the answer it gets back.

// A `secret` binding. The literal's bytes are behind a bar the moment the file
// opens, because the extension asked which ranges to conceal and was told this
// one. Hover the bar and "Reveal Secret" uncovers this range and no other, in
// this window, until you close the editor. Turn the whole behaviour off with
// `nvs.secrets.redact` if you are alone and it is in your way.
secret string $apiKey = "sk-live-not-a-real-key";

// A `tainted` binding: a value from outside that nothing has checked yet. It is
// never concealed — reading it over someone's shoulder is not an incident — and
// by default it carries only the token modifier your theme already styles. Set
// `nvs.taint.mark` to `declaration` and a glyph appears after the name.
tainted string $query = "shoes";

// Colour arrives twice on this class. The TextMate grammar in the extension had
// already coloured `final`, `class` and the string below before any process
// started; the server then said that `Receipt` is a class and `$cents` is a
// property, which is the half a regular expression cannot answer.
final class Receipt
{
    public int $cents = 0;

    // Hover `Core\Str::format` and the editor shows what it does, read out of
    // the compiler itself. There is no stub file to install.
    public function line(): string
    {
        return Core\Str::format("%d cents", $this->cents);
    }
}

var $receipt = new Receipt();
$receipt->cents = 1250;

// Nothing above ever reads `$apiKey`, and that is the point of the example: the
// concealment is about what is on screen, and the value is still the plain bytes
// in the file that the cursor, the selection and every edit address.
echo $receipt->line(), "\n";
